1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,570
On April 16, 1991, Bernie Madoff said he bought 123 call

2
00:00:05,580 --> 00:00:07,640
options on Procter and Gamble stock.

3
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:12,000
One problem, in the entire market, that whole day,

4
00:00:12,460 --> 00:00:14,740
only 20 of those options changed hands.

5
00:00:15,460 --> 00:00:20,730
He claimed 6 times more than existed, and nobody stopped him for 17 more

6
00:00:20,740 --> 00:00:25,860
years. By the time it collapsed, the fake balances said $65 billion.

7
00:00:27,320 --> 00:00:31,000
Here's the thing, that impossible number isn't a fluke.

8
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:36,020
The biggest frauds in history all hide one, a number that cannot exist.

9
00:00:36,980 --> 00:00:38,040
Today, three of them.

10
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:42,340
A coupon scam from 1920, a tank farm full of water,

11
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:44,840
and the fund that almost never lost.

12
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:48,830
And by the end, you'll have a three -question test that would have caught

13
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:52,780
all three, because the next one won't call itself a Ponzi scheme.

14
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:57,410
Chapter one, the coupon that couldn't exist.

15
00:00:57,420 --> 00:00:59,610
Boston, 1920.

16
00:00:59,620 --> 00:01:05,530
Banks are paying about 5% a year, and a former dishwasher named Charles

17
00:01:05,540 --> 00:01:09,340
Ponzi is promising 50% in 45 days.

18
00:01:09,980 --> 00:01:11,220
His pitch sounded clever.

19
00:01:12,580 --> 00:01:13,560
International reply coupons.

20
00:01:14,220 --> 00:01:17,390
Little slips you could buy cheap in Europe, where money was weak after

21
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:20,630
the war, and trade in America for stamps worth more.

22
00:01:20,640 --> 00:01:23,480
Buy low there, cash in high here.

23
00:01:24,120 --> 00:01:25,760
On paper, that's arbitrage.

24
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:26,840
It's even legal.

25
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:29,720
And for a while, it looked like it worked.

26
00:01:30,540 --> 00:01:31,720
Early investors got paid.

27
00:01:32,140 --> 00:01:33,920
On time. Word spread.

28
00:01:34,540 --> 00:01:38,500
By July, money was pouring in at close to a million dollars a day.

29
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,220
Even most of Boston's police force bought in.

30
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:43,180
But nobody did the math.

31
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:46,710
Until a financial journalist named Clarence Barron did.

32
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:52,090
To cover what Ponzi owed, 160 million postal coupons would have to be

33
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:52,700
in circulation.

34
00:01:53,380 --> 00:01:54,080
The actual number?

35
00:01:54,660 --> 00:01:56,200
About 27,000.

36
00:01:56,800 --> 00:01:58,040
That's not a little short.

37
00:01:58,420 --> 00:02:01,480
That's about one coupon for every 6,000 he'd need.

38
00:02:02,080 --> 00:02:03,700
So where were the profits coming from?

39
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:05,340
The next investor.

40
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,540
Old investors were paid with new investors' money.

41
00:02:09,020 --> 00:02:09,820
That's the whole trick.

42
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:13,930
When the papers ran the numbers, the line outside his office turned into

43
00:02:13,940 --> 00:02:16,360
a run. Six banks went down.

44
00:02:17,140 --> 00:02:18,680
Investors lost about $20 million.

45
00:02:19,460 --> 00:02:23,040
In 1920, Ponzi pleaded guilty to mail fraud.

46
00:02:23,540 --> 00:02:25,380
His name became the word for the scam.

47
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:28,800
And he died broke with an estate of $75.

48
00:02:30,280 --> 00:02:33,360
Ponzi's impossible number was hiding in coupons.

49
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:37,420
The next one was hiding in plain sight, inside a tank.

50
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:41,410
Chapter two, more oil than America.

51
00:02:41,420 --> 00:02:44,420
New Jersey. The early 1960s.

52
00:02:45,620 --> 00:02:49,500
Tino De Angelis runs a vegetable oil company called Allied Crude,

53
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:51,780
out of a run -down tank farm in Bayonne.

54
00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:54,580
His business ran on a simple kind of loan.

55
00:02:55,540 --> 00:02:56,500
Oil sits in a tank.

56
00:02:57,060 --> 00:02:59,780
A warehouse company checks the tank and writes a receipt.

57
00:03:00,580 --> 00:03:02,480
Banks lend money against the receipt.

58
00:03:03,160 --> 00:03:04,840
And when the company writing those receipts?

59
00:03:05,540 --> 00:03:07,060
A unit of American Express.

60
00:03:07,840 --> 00:03:10,970
If American Express vouched for the oil, the banks paid.

61
00:03:10,980 --> 00:03:13,440
So De Angelis made more oil.

62
00:03:14,120 --> 00:03:16,040
Not real oil, receipt oil.

63
00:03:16,760 --> 00:03:20,380
Some tanks had hidden compartments, so inspectors saw oil at the hatch.

64
00:03:21,260 --> 00:03:23,400
Others were mostly water, because oil floats.

65
00:03:24,440 --> 00:03:27,140
Inspectors looked at the top, saw oil, and signed.

66
00:03:27,760 --> 00:03:31,670
And when they walked to the next tank, pipes moved the same oil ahead

67
00:03:31,680 --> 00:03:35,380
of them. The same oil, counted again and again.

68
00:03:36,100 --> 00:03:37,370
Here's the impossible number.

69
00:03:37,380 --> 00:03:42,950
By 1963, Allied claimed more soybean and cottonseed oil than existed

70
00:03:42,960 --> 00:03:44,940
in the entire United States.

71
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:47,940
On paper, 900,000 tons.

72
00:03:48,700 --> 00:03:51,320
Actually there, about 55,000.

73
00:03:51,760 --> 00:03:54,100
Over 90% of it never existed.

74
00:03:54,940 --> 00:03:57,320
Then he tried to corner the market in oil futures.

75
00:03:58,040 --> 00:04:01,940
When prices cracked in November 1963, the margin calls came,

76
00:04:02,280 --> 00:04:04,520
and somebody finally looked inside the tanks.

77
00:04:05,420 --> 00:04:06,780
51,000 lenders.

78
00:04:07,380 --> 00:04:10,100
More than $180 million in losses.

79
00:04:10,940 --> 00:04:13,180
The American Express warehouse unit went bankrupt.

80
00:04:13,660 --> 00:04:15,280
A Wall Street brokerage went under.

81
00:04:15,940 --> 00:04:18,840
De Angelis pleaded guilty and was sentenced to 20 years.

82
00:04:19,460 --> 00:04:20,660
He was out in seven.

83
00:04:21,440 --> 00:04:22,680
Coupons, tanks.

84
00:04:23,500 --> 00:04:26,800
Both frauds needed more of something than the world actually had.

85
00:04:27,220 --> 00:04:30,500
But the biggest one in history needed something even rarer.

86
00:04:30,960 --> 00:04:32,100
A perfect record.

87
00:04:32,860 --> 00:04:36,750
Chapter three, the fund that almost never lost.

88
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:39,480
Bernie Madoff wasn't a nobody.

89
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:41,580
He'd been chairman of the NASDAQ.

90
00:04:42,140 --> 00:04:45,380
His trading firm was one of the biggest market makers on Wall Street.

91
00:04:45,960 --> 00:04:49,540
And his investment fund had a pitch that sounded almost boring.

92
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:52,680
No 50%. Just steady returns.

93
00:04:53,220 --> 00:04:54,400
Around 10% a year.

94
00:04:54,980 --> 00:04:57,080
Year after year after year.

95
00:04:57,760 --> 00:04:59,160
That was the genius of it.

96
00:04:59,780 --> 00:05:00,830
Ponzi sold greed.

97
00:05:00,840 --> 00:05:02,960
Madoff sold safety.

98
00:05:03,840 --> 00:05:08,060
One fund reported 10.5% a year for 17 years.

99
00:05:09,040 --> 00:05:13,430
In late 2008, while markets were crashing, it said it was still up for

100
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:16,500
the year. Now picture a real investment chart.

101
00:05:16,940 --> 00:05:18,280
It wiggles. It drops.

102
00:05:18,820 --> 00:05:20,600
It has bad months and bad years.

103
00:05:21,460 --> 00:05:25,540
Madoff's line went up at an almost perfect 45 degree angle.

104
00:05:26,200 --> 00:05:30,130
A financial analyst named Harry Markopolos was asked to copy Madoff's

105
00:05:30,140 --> 00:05:34,070
strategy. He later said he knew within five minutes that the numbers

106
00:05:34,080 --> 00:05:34,860
didn't add up.

107
00:05:35,340 --> 00:05:37,340
It took him four hours to prove it.

108
00:05:37,860 --> 00:05:39,280
His red flags were math.

109
00:05:40,040 --> 00:05:43,540
Madoff reported losing months only about 4% of the time.

110
00:05:44,100 --> 00:05:45,780
Real markets don't move like that.

111
00:05:46,300 --> 00:05:48,580
And the strategy itself was impossible.

112
00:05:49,260 --> 00:05:52,570
To make the trades Madoff claimed, he would have needed more options

113
00:05:52,580 --> 00:05:55,820
on the Chicago Board Options Exchange than actually existed.

114
00:05:56,460 --> 00:05:57,830
Same pattern as 1991.

115
00:05:57,840 --> 00:06:00,360
123 options claimed.

116
00:06:01,240 --> 00:06:05,260
20 traded. Markopolos took his math to the SEC in 2000.

117
00:06:05,900 --> 00:06:10,830
And again. In 2005, he sent a memo titled, The world's largest hedge

118
00:06:10,840 --> 00:06:11,960
fund is a fraud.

119
00:06:12,660 --> 00:06:17,040
Over 16 years, regulators looked at Madoff's firm at least eight times.

120
00:06:17,580 --> 00:06:18,680
They didn't find the fraud.

121
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:21,120
Then 2008 happened.

122
00:06:21,980 --> 00:06:24,500
Markets crashed and investors asked for their money back.

123
00:06:24,920 --> 00:06:26,950
About $7 billion of it.

124
00:06:26,960 --> 00:06:31,260
Madoff's bank account, which had held over $5 billion earlier that year,

125
00:06:31,620 --> 00:06:34,160
was down to about $234 million.

126
00:06:34,800 --> 00:06:38,530
On December 10th, he told his sons he was finished and that the fund

127
00:06:38,540 --> 00:06:39,680
was a Ponzi scheme.

128
00:06:40,340 --> 00:06:41,360
They went to the authorities.

129
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:43,680
The next morning, he was arrested.

130
00:06:44,780 --> 00:06:49,440
Prosecutors put the fraud at $64.8 billion based on the fake statements.

131
00:06:50,100 --> 00:06:54,550
Markopolos estimated that $35 to $55 billion of that was profit that

132
00:06:54,560 --> 00:06:55,990
never existed at all.

133
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:01,700
Madoff pleaded guilty to 11 federal crimes and was sentenced to 150 years.

134
00:07:02,320 --> 00:07:04,140
He died in prison in 2021.

135
00:07:05,540 --> 00:07:07,500
So here's the impossible number test.

136
00:07:08,060 --> 00:07:08,580
Three questions.

137
00:07:09,280 --> 00:07:10,580
They would have caught all three.

138
00:07:11,540 --> 00:07:11,940
Question one.

139
00:07:12,520 --> 00:07:14,780
Does the math need more of something than exists?

140
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:16,100
More coupons.

141
00:07:16,720 --> 00:07:18,500
More oil. More options.

142
00:07:19,220 --> 00:07:21,620
If a return needs a market bigger than the market,

143
00:07:21,940 --> 00:07:22,820
it's not a return.

144
00:07:23,640 --> 00:07:24,190
Question two.

145
00:07:24,200 --> 00:07:25,800
Is it too smooth?

146
00:07:26,440 --> 00:07:27,520
Real money wiggles.

147
00:07:28,120 --> 00:07:30,800
If something never has a bad month, that's not skill.

148
00:07:31,240 --> 00:07:31,980
That's a story.

149
00:07:32,860 --> 00:07:33,320
Question three.

150
00:07:33,860 --> 00:07:35,460
Can anyone check it besides them?

151
00:07:36,060 --> 00:07:38,000
Ponzi kept his books on index cards.

152
00:07:38,620 --> 00:07:41,020
D 'Angelo's inspectors had close ties to him.

153
00:07:41,620 --> 00:07:44,540
Madoff ran his own brokerage, so he checked his own homework.

154
00:07:45,380 --> 00:07:48,860
Three frauds, three different eras, one pattern.

155
00:07:49,260 --> 00:07:50,940
A number that can't exist.

156
00:07:51,560 --> 00:07:55,070
So next time someone shows you a perfect chart, don't ask how much you

157
00:07:55,080 --> 00:07:57,800
could make. Ask what it would take for it to be true.

158
00:07:58,540 --> 00:08:01,890
Subscribe for the next case, and tell me in the comments which red flag

159
00:08:01,900 --> 00:08:03,080
would have caught you first.

160
00:08:03,680 --> 00:08:06,240
Then, watch the tank that fooled American Express.
